      "text": "To determine the simplified form of \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\), we start by expressing the radicand in terms of its prime factors and then separate the exponent as a sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^4 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the property of exponents \\(\\sqrt[n]{a \\cdot b} = \\sqrt[n]{a} \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{b}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^4 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^4} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{3^3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we simplify each term under the fourth root:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^4} = 2^{4/4} = 2^1 = 2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^3} = 2^{3/4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{3^3} = 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nCombining these results, we have:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the terms involving \\(2\\) and \\(3\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 3)^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified form is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}}\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as \\(2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\\), where \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = 6^3 = 216\\). The sum \\(a + b\\) is:\n\n\\[\na + b = 2 + 216 = 218\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{218}\\).",
